From 2d19f75f364b782d540c3049efea332f7bfe6106 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: primez <mpincoski@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 01:35:42 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 06/12] [ADTSReader] Read nothing past a frame before the frame
 takes the PTS

AdjustStreamForPadding() called Tell() after each frame. At the segment
end, AdaptiveStream::tell() calls read(0, 0), which switches to the next
segment, and the session then sets the PTS offset of the next segment.
The reader read the PTS of the frame only after that, so the last frame
of each segment took the offset of the next segment. The PTS diff was
then one frame wrong (21-32 ms) until the next seek, and the session
uses that diff to select the segment of a seek. The E-AC-3 peek for a
dependent substream had the same Tell() at the frame end.

The parsers now compute the frame end from the frame start and size,
so nothing after the frame data can switch the segment. The next
ReadPacket() reads the next segment, and the first frame of that
segment takes the offset.

The padding skip also relied on the switch inside Tell(): 16 bytes of
padding were skipped only when the next segment was already downloaded.
AES-128 segments from a chunked download keep the PKCS#7 padding of 1
to 16 bytes, so the skip now covers up to 16 bytes in every case.

The test stream now calls the segment change observer, as
CSession::OnSegmentChanged does, and a test checks the PTS diff of
each sample across a segment change for AAC, AC-3 and E-AC-3, with
padding, and with a next segment that arrives after a wait.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
